## Set up

This lesson sets up the models and the browser extension. Compos uses three kinds of model. Set them up first, and the later lessons can use them. A lesson that needs no model works without this setup, so you can also come back here later.

| Model | What it does | Where it runs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A chat model | Writes replies in chats, and does work as an agent. | A connector, such as Claude Code, Codex or a direct API. |
| JEV | Classifies text fast and at a low cost: urgency, a team, the group of a buffer. | The TypeSafe System One API. |
| laya | Answers typed questions about a passage: a choice, a score, or a yes or no, with a confidence. | This machine, in a local daemon. |

### A chat model

> **Try it.** On the slide, choose the model that new chats use. Each card is one connector, and has one button: **Use this** when it is ready, **Install** when its program is missing, or **Add a key** when it needs one.

> **Try it.** Compos sends a short test message to the model you chose. This box fills when the reply comes back.

If no reply comes, the slide tells you what is wrong. Choose another card, or type `M-x llm-connectors` to test each connector and see its details. `M-x setup-ai-guide` explains connectors, logins and keys.

### Decisions

Compos makes small decisions with a decision model: is a mail urgent, which group does a buffer belong to, which command fits a request. **decide** asks the first model that is ready. It asks JEV when a TypeSafe key is set, and laya, on this machine, when it is not.

JEV needs a TypeSafe API key in the `TYPESAFE_API_KEY` environment variable. `JEV_API_KEY` also works. laya needs Apple silicon and a Python that has the `laya-mlx` package. The setting `laya-python` names that Python, and `M-x laya-server` starts its daemon.

> **Try it.** Compos asks **decide** a test question. This box fills when an answer comes back, and the slide says which model answered.

### The browser extension

The extension connects Compos to your Chrome tabs. With it, `M-x` and `C-x` work in any page, and Compos can read a tab, type into it, and open pages in your own browser with your logins. The extension is in the `extension` folder of the Compos checkout.

1. Open `chrome://extensions` in Chrome.
2. Turn on **Developer mode**, at the top right.
3. Click **Load unpacked**, and choose the `extension` folder.

The extension finds the daemon on port 4004 by itself. Its **Options** page shows what it is connected to.

> **Try it.** Load the extension with the steps above. This box fills by itself when the extension connects to Compos.

Then type `M-x list-tabs` to see your open Chrome tabs in Compos.

## Windows and focus

The screen has windows, and a window shows a buffer. Now you see three: the practice page, this slide, and the lesson. The **focus** is the window that your keys go to. Its title is marked.

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `s-<right>` | Move the focus to the window on the right |
| `s-<left>` | Move the focus to the window on the left |
| `C-x o` | Move the focus to the next window |
| `M-<down>` | Scroll the other window, and keep the focus here |
| `M-<up>` | Scroll the other window back |

`s-` means the Command key on a Mac, and the Super key on other computers. So `s-<right>` is Command and the right arrow.

> **Try it.** Type `s-<right>` to move the focus one window to the right. Then type `s-<left>` to come back.

> **Try it.** Type `C-x o` to move the focus to the next window.

You can read a window and not go to it. `M-<down>` scrolls the other window, and the focus stays where it is. Use it to read the lesson while you stay on the slide.

> **Try it.** Type `M-<down>` to scroll the lesson while you stay here. Then type `M-<up>` to scroll it back.

## Typing and the focus

Windows can be in one of two modes - ```focus``` or ```editing```. `focus` mode is the mode where you manipulate your window, like moving it around and so on. Most windows aren't editable so this is the default. 

A window with an editable buffer in it remains in `focus` mode until you type in it. To return to `focus` mode after typing, press ^-g / ESC 

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `ESC` or `C-g` | Stop editing, or cancel a command |
| `ESC` | The same |

When you stop editing, the arrow keys move the focus again.

> **Try it.** Move the focus to the practice page and type a word.

> **Try it.** Type `ESC` or `C-g` to stop editing. Then type `s-<right>` to go back to the slide.

## Selecting, copying and pasting

Hold Shift and move to select text. Shift and the right arrow selects one more character. Shift and the down arrow selects one more line. Cmd-A (`s-a`) selects everything.

The system keys copy and paste: Cmd-C and Cmd-V on a Mac, Ctrl-C and Ctrl-V on other computers.

> **Try it.** In the practice page, hold Shift and press the right arrow a few times to select some words. Copy them with Cmd-C.

> **Try it.** Move to the end of a line and paste with Cmd-V.

`C-w` and `C-y` do the same in the Emacs way.

## Searching and replacing

To search forward, type `C-s`. To search backward, type `C-r`. Each character you type makes the match more specific at once. Search is a movement command; it does not make you fill a form before you see a result.

> **Try it.** Type `C-s`, then type "search", and watch point move as the match becomes specific. Type `C-s` again for the next match. Type `ESC` or `C-g` to stop.

While you search, `RET` accepts the match. `ESC` or `C-g` quits and returns to where the search started, where that makes sense.

`M-%` runs `query-replace`. It asks for the old text and the new text, then visits each match, so you choose which ones to change.

> **Try it.** Put point at the start of the practice line below. Type `M-%`, replace "colour" with "color", and answer `y` for the match. Type `C-/` afterward if you want the original text back.

This practice line uses the word colour once.

## Key notation

Some commands use more than one key. A space between keys means "then", not "at the same time".

For example, `C-x C-f` means:

1. Hold CONTROL and press `x`, then release both keys.
2. Hold CONTROL and press `f`, then release both keys.

`C-x` is called a **prefix key**. It tells Compos to wait for the next key. The next key chooses the command.

Some keys are written by name:

| Key | Keyboard key |
| --- | --- |
| `RET` | Return |
| `SPC` | Space |
| `DEL` | Backspace |

Angle brackets name a key on the keyboard: `<down>`, `<up>`, `<left>` and `<right>` are the arrow keys. `s-` means hold the Super key, which is Command on macOS.

If a prefix, a prompt or a search waits for input you do not want to give, press `ESC`. It cancels whatever waits. `C-g` does the same, except after `C-x`, where `C-x C-g` starts the group commands.

> **Try it.** Hold CONTROL and press `x`, then release both keys. Compos is now waiting for the next key, and shows the keys that can follow. Press `ESC` to cancel.

You started a prefix command and cancelled it.

## Named commands

Every action in Compos is a named command. A key binding, a menu, a Scheme program, an agent, a test, or an outside client can all run the same command. The key is only one way to ask for the action.

`M-x` asks for a command by name. The list gets shorter as you type. Beside each name, the margin shows two things: the key that runs the command, and one line on what it does. So using names teaches you the keys.

> **Try it.** In the practice page, type `M-x`, then type `previous`. Read the margin beside `previous-buffer`: its key, `C-x <left>`, and "Switch to the previously used buffer". Press `RET` to run it. The page shows the buffer you used before.

> **Try it.** Type `C-x <right>` to come back to the practice page. You did not need `M-x` this time: the margin told you the key.

You never need to know a command before you use it. Type the words you would use to describe what you want.

> **Try it.** Type `M-x`, then type `new chat`. Read the margin beside **chat-new**: it starts a new chat buffer, and the margin shows its key. Press `ESC` to leave the command picker. A later lesson starts a chat.

Agents use this same list. An agent does not guess which rectangle is a button. It finds and runs the named commands you use.

